Id: sum001f
Severity: FAIL
Category: boot
Check: check_single
Fix: Set a boot loader password, or add ~~:S:wait:/sbin/sulogin to /etc/inittab.
References: sulogin(8); inittab(5)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST AC-3, IA-2; ISO 8.5, 8.9; CE Secure configuration

The system does not appear to require a password during single-user
mode boot.  Either add a password to your boot loader or add the line:
~~:S:wait:/sbin/sulogin
to your /etc/inittab file.  This line should be added immediately before
the line containing "rc 0".

Only a System V init system is judged this way: one whose inittab has
an initdefault entry and whose init is not systemd, which does not read
the inittab (its rescue and emergency services are sum002f's). Any
runlevel S entry that runs sulogin counts, and so does
SINGLE=/sbin/sulogin in /etc/sysconfig/init on Red Hat's upstart
releases.
